1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Amen. I believe that this morning.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:10,000
A reading from Acts.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,200
As for Philip, an angel of the Lord said to him,

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,720
Go south down the desert road that runs from Jerusalem to Gaza.

5
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:21,580
So he started out and he met the treasurer of Ethiopia,

6
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:26,780
a eunuch of great authority under the Kandig, the queen of Ethiopia.

7
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:31,700
The eunuch had gone to Jerusalem to worship, and he was now returning.

8
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:36,400
Seated in his carriage, he was reading aloud from the book of the prophet Isaiah.

9
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:41,780
The Holy Spirit said to Philip, Go over and walk along beside the carriage.

10
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,920
Philip ran over and heard the man reading from the prophet Isaiah.

11
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:49,240
Philip asked, Do you understand what you're reading?

12
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:53,900
The man replied, How can I, unless someone instructs me?

13
00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:57,760
And he urged Philip to come into the carriage and sit with him.

14
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:01,540
The passage of Scripture he had been reading was this.

15
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:07,520
He was led like a sheep to the slaughter, and as a lamb is silent before the shearers,

16
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,180
he did not open his mouth.

17
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,540
He was humiliated and received no justice.

18
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:14,800
Who can speak of his descendants?

19
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,460
For his life was taken from the earth.

20
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:23,800
The eunuch asked Philip, Tell me, was the prophet talking about himself or someone else?

21
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:29,200
So beginning with the same Scripture, Philip told him the good news about Jesus.

22
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:35,480
As they rode along, they came to some water, and the eunuch said, Look, there's some water.

23
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,260
Why can't I be baptized?

24
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:43,120
He ordered the carriage to stop, and they went down into the water, and Philip baptized him.

25
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:47,860
When they came up out of the water, the spirit of the Lord snatched Philip away.

26
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:52,960
The eunuch never saw him again, but went on, his way rejoicing.

27
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:56,260
You guys can have a seat.

28
00:01:56,260 --> 00:02:04,280
Thanks so much again for joining us this morning.

29
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:08,260
If you didn't catch the announcements on your way, my name is Ryan, and I have the privilege

30
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:09,500
of being one of the pastors here.

31
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:16,580
And last week, we had a really unique time of self-reflection and examination, of kind

32
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:18,780
of soul reflection, soul examination.

33
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:21,120
If you weren't here, it was special.

34
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:22,820
It was emotional.

35
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:23,860
It was unique.

36
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:27,780
I actually shed some tears with some of you last week.

37
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:30,480
It was heavy, but it was good.

38
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:38,380
And I think there's no better way to fulfill Paul's, the other half of Paul's command, to

39
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:42,880
weep with those who weep, and to rejoice with those who rejoice, than to follow up last week's

40
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:52,420
kind of emotional soul reflection that might have led some of us to shed some tears with

41
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:54,420
the time of joy.

42
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:57,980
And I think it's a time of rejoicing this morning in baptism, the time of celebrating new faith

43
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:59,020
in Jesus.

44
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:06,260
And I think the family of God really was designed to teach us, to give us a healthy perspective,

45
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:09,620
a well-rounded perspective on the realities of life.

46
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:15,600
Because the reality is, all of us know, if we've lived long enough, that we have peaks and valleys.

47
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,400
We have mountaintops, and we find ourselves in valleys.

48
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:24,560
And I think the family of God and us coming together on a weekly basis to spend time with one another, to

49
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:30,860
encourage one another, to hear Scriptures and to worship reminds us of this well-rounded perspective of life.

50
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:36,580
The reality is, there's some of you in this room that are really healthy, and some of you that are sick.

51
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:39,440
There's people at home watching that are sick.

52
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:45,920
There's some of you that have been to funerals in the last few weeks, while others have celebrated the birth of new babies.

53
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:52,500
There's some of you that are walking through the difficulty of broken relationships, while others have celebrated

54
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:55,120
anniversaries and marriages recently.

55
00:03:55,120 --> 00:04:01,720
See, all throughout the body of Christ, we have people all over that spectrum of mountaintops and valleys.

56
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:08,340
And I really just can't think of a better way to follow up last week's time of soul reflection and weeping than to rejoice

57
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:12,480
with those who are rejoicing this morning as we celebrate baptism.

58
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:14,000
So that's kind of where we're going to head this morning.

59
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,460
I'm super excited about it.

60
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:19,720
Let me pray, and then we'll kind of hop into that text that Shauna read for us a minute ago.

61
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:29,200
Father, we're so thankful that you have given us the family of God to remind us of your faithfulness and your goodness,

62
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:35,060
both when we find ourselves on the mountaintop and when we find ourselves in the valley

63
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:38,000
and when we find ourselves everywhere in between.

64
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,040
You are faithful.

65
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:40,380
You are good.

66
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:43,880
You are present and active in our life.

67
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:47,400
And you have brought this family of God around us.

68
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:51,720
Some of us are in here with little to no physical family left.

69
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:59,500
But you've given us the church as a spiritual family, a spiritual heritage that we can rally around one another

70
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:04,520
and we can celebrate when others are celebrating and we can mourn when others are mourning.

71
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:07,960
So I'm so glad that you've given us the church this morning.

72
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:15,020
I pray for each of us as we hear from your word this morning, as we worship, as we spend time in the lobby and fellowship,

73
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:22,060
I pray that every element of this experience today would be used by your Holy Spirit to draw us to yourself,

74
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:27,940
to point us to what you want us to hear, to encourage us to do what you want us to do.

75
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:36,160
God, I pray that this message on baptism would be a reminder for all of us of what our responsibility is as followers of Jesus.

76
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:39,460
And I pray all of this in Jesus' name.

77
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:40,880
Amen.

78
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:44,300
So if you have your Bibles, you can turn to Acts chapter 8.

79
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:48,320
We're going to work through that story that Shauna read just a minute ago.

80
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:54,940
And as you're turning there, I'd love to kind of do a thought exercise with us this morning.

81
00:05:54,940 --> 00:06:02,120
How many of you have ever found yourself staring at something, a simple puzzle or a simple math problem

82
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:07,260
where you keep going through it in your head and maybe you keep getting the wrong answer and it shouldn't be that difficult?

83
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:11,860
Maybe one of those Facebook puzzles where, have you ever seen the ones where it's like a full screen of eights

84
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:16,800
and it's like find the one S in the eight and you're like staring at it for like ten minutes and you're like,

85
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:21,780
huh, is there actually an S in this puzzle or is the person like pranking me?

86
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:26,120
Have you ever gotten stuck like just staring at something that shouldn't be that complicated

87
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:28,880
but ultimately ends up kind of messing with your brain?

88
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:34,200
Or I remember a few years ago, there was this dress that was very famous on social media.

89
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:36,060
I think I have a picture of it.

90
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:37,500
Do you guys remember this dress?

91
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:40,500
Anybody remember the fights that they got in their friends' list over it?

92
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:43,320
Is this dress blue and black or is it white and gold?

93
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:44,860
If you see white and gold, raise your hands.

94
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:49,480
Those of you at home, we got probably 20% of the room says white and gold.

95
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:50,340
What about blue and black?

96
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:54,500
A majority of the room sees blue and black.

97
00:06:54,500 --> 00:07:00,800
What really messes with you is when it starts flip-flopping, when it's white and gold and then blue and black

98
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:02,600
while you're looking at it at the same time.

99
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:10,540
But the reason I bring this up is because sometimes, sometimes we get stuck in a perspective.

100
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:15,920
We get stuck looking at something for so long or seeing something the same way for so long

101
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:20,800
that it's impossible for us to really step back and to look at something with a different perspective.

102
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:27,480
Sometimes if we're not careful, we're stubborn people and we get stuck focused on one perspective

103
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:31,200
without really having kind of a holistic perspective.

104
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,300
We talk about this with communion a lot, right?

105
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:35,660
We take communion almost every single week here at Canvas.

106
00:07:35,660 --> 00:07:41,100
And so sometimes it's easy for us to just get in the rhythm or the routine of doing this thing

107
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:46,320
that God commanded us to do without really understanding the beauty and significance of it.

108
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:49,840
And I think the same can be true of baptism.

109
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:53,120
We can just get so used to seeing it.

110
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:56,740
For those of us that are insiders in the room, those of us that grew up in church our whole lives,

111
00:07:56,740 --> 00:07:59,740
we've seen hundreds of baptisms our entire life.

112
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:01,100
We've seen this over and over again.

113
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:05,640
And if we're not careful, it can just become normal, routine, just something that we do,

114
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:13,180
a tradition that we participate in because God told us to and it's cool and fun and we clap.

115
00:08:13,180 --> 00:08:17,860
But if we're not careful, we just get stuck looking at it that way.

116
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:18,820
Or maybe you're an outsider.

117
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:19,940
Maybe you didn't grow up in the church.

118
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:22,800
You're kind of just kicking the tires on this Christianity thing,

119
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:25,600
trying to figure out what the claims of Jesus really are all about.

120
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:30,740
And when you think about somebody getting in a lukewarm bath tank in front of a bunch of people

121
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:32,880
and going underwater, you might look at that.

122
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:34,880
You might have a perspective like that's weird.

123
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:37,160
That's a ritualistic religious thing.

124
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:39,040
And you might not understand it.

125
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:44,060
Well, wherever you find yourself on that spectrum of perspective about baptism,

126
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:49,260
I think it's really good before we celebrate people going public in baptism

127
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:54,460
to kind of take a step back, to look at baptism with a fresh perspective.

128
00:08:54,460 --> 00:09:00,020
And I don't think there's any better story in Scripture to take a fresh look at baptism

129
00:09:00,020 --> 00:09:02,900
than the story of Philip baptizing the eunuch.

130
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:10,280
Like we have the process of baptism from beginning to end all in just a few verses here in Acts.

131
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:14,220
So we're going to just work through that and really look at a fresh perspective on baptism.

132
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:18,960
My hope is by the end of this that if you're an insider, you've been in church your whole life,

133
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:20,740
you've seen a hundred different baptisms.

134
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:26,020
I hope that before we get to the point where we're going to baptize some people at the end of service,

135
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:30,240
that you're reminded of the beauty and the significance of baptism

136
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:34,440
and that you should be a part of baptisms as a believer.

137
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:38,100
And if you're an outsider and you're new to this whole Christianity thing,

138
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:43,740
I hope this gives you a pretty good perspective about what we believe about baptism here at Canvas.

139
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:45,800
So as we hop into Acts chapter 8,

140
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:51,040
I'm going to keep hammering in this one main point that I believe is true about baptism,

141
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:56,200
that baptism is the culmination of a believer's obedience.

142
00:09:56,200 --> 00:10:00,360
Baptism is the culmination of a believer's obedience.

143
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:04,520
And that word believers, that is both plural and possessive.

144
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:09,740
Because not only is the person that's stepping into the tank to get baptized,

145
00:10:09,740 --> 00:10:11,220
are they being obedient?

146
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:15,780
But that step of obedience is built on other believers around them,

147
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:18,520
being obedient to do the thing that God called them to do,

148
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:21,780
which is to go into all the world and make disciples,

149
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:26,040
baptizing them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.

150
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:28,620
And so when we see somebody get baptized this morning,

151
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:30,660
we're not just seeing them take a step of obedience,

152
00:10:30,660 --> 00:10:34,820
but we're seeing people around them who are followers of Jesus in their life,

153
00:10:34,820 --> 00:10:38,020
who have sowed seeds of gospel into their life,

154
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:40,300
who have encouraged them, who have told them about Jesus,

155
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:43,300
who have reminded them about the faithfulness of God in their life.

156
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:48,420
Baptism is a culmination of the believer's obedience.

157
00:10:48,420 --> 00:10:50,200
And that's why at Canvas, oftentimes,

158
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,380
the pastors aren't the ones doing the baptizing,

159
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:54,280
because oftentimes, at Canvas,

160
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:57,500
the people that are getting baptized have been far more spiritually influenced

161
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:01,800
by the priests in their life rather than the pastors on the stage.

162
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:03,160
And we think that that's a good thing.

163
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:07,820
We want you to remember that you are a priest to those around you,

164
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:11,740
and that one day you might find yourself down here baptizing somebody in your life

165
00:11:11,740 --> 00:11:15,260
because you were faithful to do the thing that God called you to do.

166
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:19,320
See, baptism is the culmination of the believer's obedience.

167
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:23,000
We're going to see that all throughout Acts chapter 8.

168
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,800
And as we hop in, I really want to give us some context,

169
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:29,080
because I think it's important when you just pick a random chapter in the middle of a book

170
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:31,340
that you have the context around it.

171
00:11:31,340 --> 00:11:36,840
So what happens at the beginning of Acts is Jesus' last words to his disciples.

172
00:11:36,840 --> 00:11:41,680
Many of us are familiar with it in Matthew chapter 28 that I just quoted, right?

173
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:42,700
Go make disciples.

174
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:44,980
But before that, he tells them,

175
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:51,900
you're going to be my witnesses in Jerusalem, Judea, Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.

176
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:56,420
You're going to go from Jerusalem to Judea to Samaria to the rest of the world

177
00:11:56,420 --> 00:12:00,780
baptizing people in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.

178
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:04,200
And they begin to be obedient to that mission in Jerusalem.

179
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:07,820
They start preaching the gospel of Jesus all throughout the city of Jerusalem,

180
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:09,480
and the church is exploding.

181
00:12:09,480 --> 00:12:10,500
People are getting saved.

182
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:15,860
Acts chapter 2 says that over 2,000 are added to the family of faith in one day.

183
00:12:15,940 --> 00:12:18,320
The church is exploding in Jerusalem.

184
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:25,940
But believers get comfortable being obedient, doing the thing that God called them to do

185
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:28,020
in the place that's most comfortable for them to do it.

186
00:12:28,020 --> 00:12:30,060
And they just kind of are partially obedient.

187
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:32,980
They're just preaching the gospel and baptizing believers in Jerusalem.

188
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:36,040
They're not really worried about Judea and Samaria because that would take effort

189
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:39,740
for them to step out of their comfort zone and to go to a place that they're not comfortable with.

190
00:12:39,740 --> 00:12:42,320
So they kind of get content with partial obedience.

191
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:44,820
And I know we would never do that today.

192
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:49,340
We never just become partially obedient.

193
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:52,760
We never do the thing that God called us to do where it's most comfortable,

194
00:12:52,760 --> 00:12:55,160
and we see the blessing of that, and we're like,

195
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:58,400
I'm just going to firmly plant myself here where I'm comfortable

196
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:01,640
and do the little obedient things that are easy for me to do

197
00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:03,480
and not worry about stepping out of my comfort zone.

198
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:06,660
I know that we would never do that, but the early church was doing that.

199
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:10,700
And they were obedient in Jerusalem, but they weren't going out.

200
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:13,160
And so God did what God often does,

201
00:13:13,980 --> 00:13:17,360
and he forces them to be obedient even if they're not ready for it.

202
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:20,120
So there's this persecution that arises in the church,

203
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:25,880
and it gets to the point where one of the deacons of the early church was murdered.

204
00:13:25,880 --> 00:13:29,800
He was killed for preaching the gospel in Jerusalem.

205
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:32,200
And the gospel that they were preaching was kind of offensive.

206
00:13:32,280 --> 00:13:37,360
The gospel that they were preaching was this Messiah whom you killed.

207
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:40,360
They're preaching this message to the religious leaders.

208
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:44,340
So you can see why they might be offended by that message, this Messiah whom you killed.

209
00:13:44,340 --> 00:13:46,560
The reality is we're going to get to this in a little bit,

210
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:48,780
but that's the same gospel that we still preach today.

211
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:52,260
Like our sin took Jesus to the cross.

212
00:13:52,260 --> 00:13:55,200
We're the ones who crucified the Messiah.

213
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,360
So they're preaching this message, and the church gets to send it in Jerusalem.

214
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:00,940
And they kill Stephen.

215
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:06,720
And this persecution forces the church out of Jerusalem into Judea and Samaria.

216
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:10,400
And so now they're being obedient to the thing that God called them to,

217
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:15,040
but it almost took God forcing them out of their comfort zone in order to be obedient.

218
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:17,080
I'm sure none of us have ever experienced that either, right,

219
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:20,460
where God forced us out of our comfort zone because we were being disobedient.

220
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:23,120
So this is kind of where we find ourselves.

221
00:14:23,120 --> 00:14:27,380
And Philip is one of the early church leaders who got forced out of Jerusalem,

222
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:29,580
and he finds himself in Samaria,

223
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:33,640
and he is having an incredibly successful ministry in Samaria.

224
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:35,760
He's healing the sick.

225
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:38,280
He's healing the blind.

226
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:43,160
He's preaching the message of Jesus, and the gospel is exploding in Samaria.

227
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:47,820
So now we have Philip being obedient to what God has called him to do

228
00:14:47,820 --> 00:14:51,660
and seeing success happen in his ministry.

229
00:14:51,660 --> 00:14:54,580
He's doing exactly what God's calling him to do,

230
00:14:54,580 --> 00:14:58,820
so much so that Peter and John, like the two, like, bosses of the early church,

231
00:14:58,820 --> 00:15:02,700
they come out to see Philip, and they're like, this guy is crushing it.

232
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:06,040
He's doing the thing that God called him to do in a place that's difficult.

233
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:10,400
Samaria was culturally different than what the Jews were comfortable with.

234
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:15,040
And so Philip is doing the thing God called him to do in a place where it is difficult,

235
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:16,420
and he's seeing success.

236
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:21,240
And that's where we get to this passage in verse 26 of chapter 8.

237
00:15:21,240 --> 00:15:22,520
He's having success.

238
00:15:22,520 --> 00:15:24,280
He's preaching the gospel in Samaria.

239
00:15:24,280 --> 00:15:26,880
And it says this in verse 26,

240
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:30,760
As for Philip, an angel of the Lord said to him,

241
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:36,960
Go south down to the desert road that runs from Jerusalem to Gaza.

242
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:40,040
The ESV calls it a desert place.

243
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:43,800
So he started out, and he met the treasurer of Ethiopia,

244
00:15:43,800 --> 00:15:47,740
a eunuch of great authority under the Kandake, the queen of Ethiopia.

245
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:50,900
The eunuch had gone to Jerusalem to worship,

246
00:15:50,900 --> 00:15:53,920
and he was now returning seated in his carriage,

247
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:57,040
and he was reading aloud from the prophet Isaiah.

248
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:01,720
So we have Philip being very successful in ministry,

249
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:03,640
doing the thing that God's calling him to do.

250
00:16:03,640 --> 00:16:05,840
And God comes to him and says,

251
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:09,040
Hey, I know that you just moved out of Jerusalem into Samaria,

252
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:10,060
and you're having success,

253
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:12,080
and you're doing the thing that I called you to do,

254
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:14,780
but now I want you to go to a desert road.

255
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:16,860
Like, that's tough, right?

256
00:16:16,960 --> 00:16:19,260
Like, have we ever heard God speak to us

257
00:16:19,260 --> 00:16:20,740
when we're in the middle of doing something

258
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:23,220
that's maybe successful and comfortable,

259
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:25,040
and we're like, I think I heard wrong.

260
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:27,900
Like, I don't think I heard you say what I think I heard you say

261
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:29,760
because I'm doing the thing that you told me to do.

262
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:32,960
Like, I was there in Jerusalem when you said,

263
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:34,980
Go to Jerusalem, Judea, Samaria,

264
00:16:34,980 --> 00:16:36,880
preach the gospel, baptize believers.

265
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:39,980
I'm in Samaria, preaching the gospel, baptizing believers.

266
00:16:39,980 --> 00:16:42,160
But God tells Philip,

267
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:44,080
Hey, I have an assignment for you.

268
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:45,640
I have a thing for you to do.

269
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,440
And it might not be easy.

270
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:51,320
It's going to take you into a desert place.

271
00:16:51,320 --> 00:16:55,920
So I think the first point that kind of hammers home this idea

272
00:16:55,920 --> 00:16:59,460
that baptism is a culmination of the obedient believer

273
00:16:59,460 --> 00:17:03,760
is that baptism starts within obedient believer.

274
00:17:03,760 --> 00:17:07,580
As we look at this process of baptism from beginning to end,

275
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:12,440
we're going to see that Philip starts by being obedient.

276
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:16,280
See, everything is going great with Philip.

277
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:19,340
He's finally at the place that God called him to do,

278
00:17:19,340 --> 00:17:21,560
and he's having success in ministry,

279
00:17:21,560 --> 00:17:25,300
and Jesus says, Go to a desert place.

280
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:29,940
And so if we think about our own baptism,

281
00:17:29,940 --> 00:17:33,400
those of us that are in the room that have gone public with our faith in Jesus,

282
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:34,860
if we think about our own baptism,

283
00:17:34,860 --> 00:17:39,020
the reality is that baptism started with an obedient believer.

284
00:17:39,320 --> 00:17:43,720
It started with somebody who stepped out of their comfort zone to share the message of Jesus with us,

285
00:17:43,720 --> 00:17:46,220
to encourage us to follow Jesus.

286
00:17:46,220 --> 00:17:51,160
And so baptism always starts with an obedient believer doing the thing that God called them to do.

287
00:17:51,160 --> 00:17:54,240
Each of us are priests where we find ourselves,

288
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:57,440
and God has called us to go into all the world

289
00:17:57,440 --> 00:18:02,900
and to preach the message of Jesus and to baptize people in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.

290
00:18:02,980 --> 00:18:07,200
And so in order for baptism to happen, it has to start with some believer who's willing to say,

291
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:12,600
I'm going to go where God tells me to go, and I'm going to do what God tells me to do.

292
00:18:12,600 --> 00:18:15,500
And so if you're in here, and you're a follower of Jesus,

293
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:21,880
I want to encourage you to build that practice into your life

294
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:25,900
where you can listen to the Holy Spirit enough that you know when he's calling you to go somewhere,

295
00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:29,160
that you know when he's calling you to step out of your comfort zone

296
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:32,620
and to plant a gospel seed in the life of somebody around you.

297
00:18:32,620 --> 00:18:36,960
Because Philip was having a successful ministry in a comfortable place for him

298
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:40,140
because the gospel was exploding, and Jesus says,

299
00:18:40,140 --> 00:18:43,920
now I need you to take a break from this and go to a desert place.

300
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:49,260
And the only way he knows that is if he's willing and intentional in his relationship with God

301
00:18:49,260 --> 00:18:51,780
to listen to the Holy Spirit prompting in his life.

302
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:57,480
Baptisms don't happen without an obedient believer stepping out in faith

303
00:18:57,480 --> 00:18:59,960
to do the thing that God called them to do.

304
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:03,040
And we know this, right?

305
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:06,760
Like we know that it's easy for us to come up with excuses.

306
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:11,220
How many times have we heard from God calling us to do something,

307
00:19:11,220 --> 00:19:14,560
to step out of our comfort zone, to go over to our neighbor's house,

308
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:17,380
to build a relationship, to step out of our comfort zone,

309
00:19:17,380 --> 00:19:19,520
to have a conversation with our coworkers about Jesus,

310
00:19:19,520 --> 00:19:23,220
to step out of our comfort zone, to ask somebody how we can pray for them.

311
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:26,560
How often have we had God prompt us to do something like that,

312
00:19:26,560 --> 00:19:29,840
and we said, man, that would be awkward.

313
00:19:29,840 --> 00:19:31,740
That would be uncomfortable.

314
00:19:31,740 --> 00:19:32,800
That would be weird.

315
00:19:32,800 --> 00:19:39,380
How often do we make excuses to not do the thing that God is calling us to do?

316
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:43,680
I think if we're honest with ourselves, it happens all the time.

317
00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:45,200
I think about Jonah, right?

318
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:50,800
Like God said, Jonah, you're going to go preach my message to the Ninevites.

319
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:52,680
And he said, no, thank you.

320
00:19:52,680 --> 00:19:54,400
I'd rather not, right?

321
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:56,120
And he ran in the opposite direction.

322
00:19:56,120 --> 00:20:00,260
How often do we come up with excuses just like Jonah to run in the opposite direction?

323
00:20:00,340 --> 00:20:02,700
But for those of you that have been following Jesus long enough,

324
00:20:02,700 --> 00:20:06,760
you know that you can keep saying no as much as you want,

325
00:20:06,760 --> 00:20:09,860
but eventually God's going to get you to a point where you're going to be obedient.

326
00:20:09,860 --> 00:20:13,180
So why not just be obedient immediately, right?

327
00:20:13,180 --> 00:20:18,280
That baptism starts with believers being obedient to do what God's calling them to do.

328
00:20:18,280 --> 00:20:23,880
And Philip, I think Philip learned this lesson when they were forced into Samaria

329
00:20:23,880 --> 00:20:25,540
because of the persecution of the church.

330
00:20:25,540 --> 00:20:30,560
Because we see as we go on, not only does Philip go to the desert place,

331
00:20:30,560 --> 00:20:35,020
but he sees this Ethiopian guy who's coming down from Jerusalem.

332
00:20:35,020 --> 00:20:36,780
This is important detail to note.

333
00:20:36,780 --> 00:20:39,880
But this Ethiopian guy was coming back from Jerusalem.

334
00:20:39,880 --> 00:20:41,000
What's happening in Jerusalem?

335
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,400
The church is being persecuted.

336
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:48,740
The message that Philip is called to preach is actually a bad message in Jerusalem.

337
00:20:48,740 --> 00:20:53,040
So he's coming back from Jerusalem, and he's seated in his carriage,

338
00:20:53,120 --> 00:20:55,980
and he's reading from the prophet Isaiah, and listen in verse 29.

339
00:20:55,980 --> 00:20:56,380
It says,

340
00:20:56,380 --> 00:20:58,280
The Holy Spirit said to Philip,

341
00:20:58,280 --> 00:21:02,600
Go over and walk along beside the carriage.

342
00:21:02,600 --> 00:21:03,560
Verse 30,

343
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:04,760
Philip ran.

344
00:21:04,760 --> 00:21:08,440
I think Philip's gotten to the point where he learned this lesson,

345
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:11,100
that when God calls you to do something, you just do it.

346
00:21:11,100 --> 00:21:14,760
Like he was there in Jerusalem dragging his feet,

347
00:21:14,760 --> 00:21:19,300
not wanting to move out of Jerusalem into Samaria or Judea or to the ends of the earth.

348
00:21:19,300 --> 00:21:22,300
And ultimately, because the church was disobedient,

349
00:21:22,620 --> 00:21:25,880
God brought persecution upon them that forced them to be obedient.

350
00:21:25,880 --> 00:21:27,340
Same thing happened to Jonah, right?

351
00:21:27,340 --> 00:21:29,280
Like Jonah didn't want to listen to God,

352
00:21:29,280 --> 00:21:33,640
and ultimately he ended in the belly of a whale and still ended up doing the thing that God called him to do.

353
00:21:33,640 --> 00:21:36,660
So Philip's gotten to this point where he's learned this lesson,

354
00:21:36,660 --> 00:21:39,620
that when God tells you to do something, you just do it.

355
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:44,220
I was talking to an old saint a few months ago,

356
00:21:44,220 --> 00:21:45,020
and she said,

357
00:21:45,020 --> 00:21:49,680
I've been a follower of Jesus long enough to know that when God tells you to do something,

358
00:21:49,680 --> 00:21:51,120
you just do it.

359
00:21:51,120 --> 00:21:53,600
How often do we make excuses?

360
00:21:53,600 --> 00:21:58,400
How often do we miss out on gospel opportunities because we are tired?

361
00:22:00,400 --> 00:22:06,480
Because we don't want to step into an awkward, uncomfortable situation because we don't want to offend anyone.

362
00:22:06,480 --> 00:22:09,060
We don't want to tell anyone something they don't want to hear.

363
00:22:09,060 --> 00:22:15,680
How often do we miss out on the beginning of a beautiful baptism process because we're not willing to step out in obedience?

364
00:22:16,960 --> 00:22:19,600
That baptism starts with an obedient believer.

365
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:27,900
So we have Philip twice obeying God to go to the desert place and then to go into the situation that's probably pretty uncomfortable, right?

366
00:22:27,900 --> 00:22:32,080
Like he's going up to a very powerful person who's just coming back from Jerusalem,

367
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:36,960
a place where they are persecuting Christians for preaching the message of Jesus.

368
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:44,160
But Philip ran over, and he heard the man reading from the prophet Isaiah.

369
00:22:45,300 --> 00:22:48,660
Philip asked him, Do you understand what you were reading?

370
00:22:48,660 --> 00:22:52,720
Then the man replied, How can I unless someone instructs me?

371
00:22:52,720 --> 00:22:56,200
And he urged Philip to come up into the carriage and sit with him.

372
00:22:56,200 --> 00:22:59,020
And the passage of Scripture that he was reading was this.

373
00:22:59,020 --> 00:23:02,880
He was led like a sheep to the slaughter.

374
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:07,020
And as a lamb is silent before the seers, he did not open his mouth.

375
00:23:07,020 --> 00:23:09,620
He was humiliated and received no justice.

376
00:23:09,620 --> 00:23:11,300
Who can speak of his descendants?

377
00:23:11,300 --> 00:23:14,620
For his life was taken from the earth.

378
00:23:15,300 --> 00:23:20,740
So because Philip was willing to be obedient, to go do the thing that God called him to do,

379
00:23:20,740 --> 00:23:28,640
God set up this incredibly perfect opportunity for him to preach the message of Jesus to this very important person.

380
00:23:28,640 --> 00:23:32,220
Like what are the odds that this guy has a scroll?

381
00:23:32,220 --> 00:23:34,580
Scrolls are very hard to come by in this time.

382
00:23:34,580 --> 00:23:40,400
And the eunuch just got a copy of Isaiah, and he just happens to be reading it in his car.

383
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:49,280
And in Isaiah 53, this passage that they're reading is literally the clearest explanation of the message of Jesus in the entire Old Testament.

384
00:23:49,280 --> 00:23:53,780
John MacArthur calls Isaiah 53 the Romans road of the Old Testament.

385
00:23:53,780 --> 00:23:58,500
It's been historically called a torture chamber for the rabbis.

386
00:23:59,060 --> 00:24:06,980
Because Jewish leaders don't believe that Jesus is the Messiah, and then they also believe that Isaiah 53 is a Scripture about the Messiah.

387
00:24:06,980 --> 00:24:17,800
And they read it, and it so clearly speaks to the message of Jesus that it's hard for them to reconcile what this passage could mean without it being about Jesus.

388
00:24:17,800 --> 00:24:24,320
So God sets up this perfect gospel moment for Philip, which brings me to my next point, which is this,

389
00:24:24,320 --> 00:24:31,820
that baptism not only does it start with an obedient believer, but it's built on a clear understanding of the message of Jesus.

390
00:24:31,820 --> 00:24:39,940
Like when we baptize people later this service, what we're doing is they are saying,

391
00:24:39,940 --> 00:24:46,940
I understand the message of Jesus, I understand that I have sinned, that I have crucified the Messiah,

392
00:24:46,940 --> 00:24:51,780
that Jesus had to come and live a perfect life and die in my place for my sins.

393
00:24:51,780 --> 00:24:57,580
And I have placed my faith and trust in that, and I'm going to picture that with my body.

394
00:24:57,580 --> 00:25:04,340
I'm going to go under the water saying that I have died to my old self, and I'm being raised to new life in Christ.

395
00:25:04,340 --> 00:25:08,600
See, baptism is built on a clear understanding of the message of Jesus.

396
00:25:08,880 --> 00:25:13,440
So God sets up this perfect moment for Philip.

397
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:17,000
He just so happens to have a scroll from Isaiah.

398
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:22,420
He just so happens to be reading the most clear passage about Jesus in the entire Old Testament.

399
00:25:22,420 --> 00:25:28,000
And not only that, but he's also interested in having a conversation with a stranger about that passage of Scripture, right?

400
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:32,640
He's like, hey, why don't you get in my car and you tell me what this passage is all about?

401
00:25:32,640 --> 00:25:34,540
God has set up this moment.

402
00:25:34,540 --> 00:25:35,980
And why did he set it up?

403
00:25:35,980 --> 00:25:38,780
He set it up because Philip was willing to be obedient.

404
00:25:39,320 --> 00:25:45,840
How often do we miss out on opportunities like this because we make excuses to not do the thing that we know the Holy Spirit is calling us to do?

405
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:56,620
And if we'd be obedient, I can't imagine how many stories we'd have like this, how many stories we'd have where God orchestrated a moment for us, but we made an excuse and missed out on it.

406
00:25:56,620 --> 00:26:08,060
And I know in my own life, I'm sure I've missed out on so many opportunities because I heard the Holy Spirit calling me to step out of my comfort zone, to go to my neighbor's house, to have a conversation with the person at the coffee shop, to do this thing.

407
00:26:08,260 --> 00:26:10,020
And I wasn't willing to do it.

408
00:26:10,020 --> 00:26:13,560
Philip has learned the lesson that it's better to just be obedient.

409
00:26:13,560 --> 00:26:22,780
He steps out of his comfort zone and he's found this perfect opportunity to preach the message of Jesus from Isaiah 53.

410
00:26:22,780 --> 00:26:31,100
Let's work through that quickly so we can see how clearly the gospel is found as he preaches this message of Jesus too.

411
00:26:31,100 --> 00:26:35,540
This Ethiopian eunuch, Isaiah 53, says,

412
00:26:35,540 --> 00:26:42,080
There was nothing beautiful or majestic about his appearance, nothing to attract us to him.

413
00:26:42,080 --> 00:26:47,040
He was despised and rejected, a man of sorrows, acquainted with deep grief.

414
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:51,560
I can't help but think about Philip telling this eunuch.

415
00:26:51,560 --> 00:26:59,280
Philip probably, knowing Jesus, seeing him in person, saying there wasn't really anything that special about him.

416
00:26:59,280 --> 00:27:00,880
He wasn't that great looking of a guy.

417
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:01,920
He was homeless.

418
00:27:02,860 --> 00:27:10,360
We followed him around and he was sleeping in tents, like he's a man of sorrows, acquainted with deep grief.

419
00:27:10,360 --> 00:27:12,040
His family turned their back on him.

420
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:15,460
We turned our backs on him and looked the other way.

421
00:27:15,460 --> 00:27:16,620
He was despised.

422
00:27:16,620 --> 00:27:18,180
We did not care.

423
00:27:18,180 --> 00:27:21,740
Yet it was our weakness he carried.

424
00:27:21,740 --> 00:27:23,900
It was our sorrows that weighed him down.

425
00:27:23,900 --> 00:27:29,640
Think about Philip sharing the message of Jesus, the eunuch, through this passage.

426
00:27:31,040 --> 00:27:33,280
But he was pierced for our rebellion.

427
00:27:33,280 --> 00:27:35,020
He was crushed for our sins.

428
00:27:35,020 --> 00:27:37,120
He was beaten so that we could be whole.

429
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:39,020
He was whipped so that we could be healed.

430
00:27:39,020 --> 00:27:44,300
Philip telling the story of Jesus going to the cross, willingly giving up his life,

431
00:27:44,300 --> 00:27:50,340
willingly being led to the cross to be crucified on behalf of us.

432
00:27:50,340 --> 00:27:52,140
And then, verse 6, look at this.

433
00:27:52,140 --> 00:27:54,160
All of us like sheep, we have strayed away.

434
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:57,440
We have left God's path and gone on our own.

435
00:27:57,440 --> 00:28:00,400
And yet the Lord laid on him the sins of us all.

436
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:05,460
I can't help but think Philip saying, hey, you just came back from Jerusalem where they were making animal sacrifices.

437
00:28:05,460 --> 00:28:07,460
They were sacrificing lambs.

438
00:28:07,460 --> 00:28:13,460
And that is a picture of who Jesus would come to be, that we are those lambs who have gone astray.

439
00:28:13,460 --> 00:28:19,900
We've all made decisions that have led us to be in direct rebellion to God.

440
00:28:19,900 --> 00:28:25,620
And yet God chose to send Jesus to be the perfect once and for all sacrifice for our sins.

441
00:28:25,620 --> 00:28:27,020
Jesus was that lamb.

442
00:28:27,020 --> 00:28:28,820
The temple is no longer needed.

443
00:28:28,820 --> 00:28:35,280
The curtain has been torn in two because Jesus is the lamb of God that takes away the sins of the world.

444
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:38,900
How clearly the message of Jesus is found in Isaiah 53.

445
00:28:40,240 --> 00:28:43,120
He was oppressed and treated harshly, yet he never said a word.

446
00:28:43,120 --> 00:28:45,300
He was led like a lamb to the slaughter.

447
00:28:45,300 --> 00:28:49,220
And as a sheep is silent before the shears, he did not open his mouth.

448
00:28:49,220 --> 00:28:51,600
Unjustly condemned, he was led away.

449
00:28:51,600 --> 00:28:59,300
No one cared that he died without descendants, that his life was cut short and midstream.

450
00:28:59,300 --> 00:29:03,300
He was struck down for the rebellion of my people.

451
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:09,260
He had done no wrong and never deceived anyone, but he was buried like a criminal.

452
00:29:09,260 --> 00:29:12,180
He was put in a rich man's grave.

453
00:29:12,180 --> 00:29:16,000
So we see this message of Jesus, this substitutionary atonement message,

454
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:20,240
that Jesus is the lamb of God who comes and dies in our place for our sins.

455
00:29:20,240 --> 00:29:23,020
But it also has the resurrection prophesied about here.

456
00:29:23,020 --> 00:29:28,360
Isaiah 53 was written 700 years before Jesus would come.

457
00:29:28,360 --> 00:29:32,540
And the prophet Isaiah says that he was put in a rich man's grave.

458
00:29:33,660 --> 00:29:38,640
People that were crucified, people that were tortured, people that were treated like this in Isaiah 53,

459
00:29:38,640 --> 00:29:40,720
they don't get buried in a rich man's grave.

460
00:29:40,720 --> 00:29:42,800
They get thrown in a pile of other bodies.

461
00:29:42,800 --> 00:29:49,300
Like if Jesus' crucifixion went the way that every other crucifixion happened in Rome,

462
00:29:49,300 --> 00:29:52,600
they would have just pulled him off the cross and thrown him in a pile of other bodies.

463
00:29:52,600 --> 00:30:01,440
But Isaiah prophesying about the coming Messiah, Jesus says that he will be buried in a rich man's tomb 700 years before we know that that happened,

464
00:30:01,440 --> 00:30:08,380
that a rich follower of Jesus paid for a brand new grave to be bought so that Jesus could be buried in it.

465
00:30:08,380 --> 00:30:10,540
And he says,

466
00:30:10,580 --> 00:30:13,620
But it was the Lord's good plan to crush him and cause him grief.

467
00:30:13,620 --> 00:30:19,460
Yet when his life is made an offering for sin, he will have many descendants.

468
00:30:19,460 --> 00:30:23,680
He will enjoy a long life.

469
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:27,080
This passage has all of the elements of the gospel.

470
00:30:27,080 --> 00:30:32,020
Jesus came, lived a perfect life in our place for our sins.

471
00:30:32,220 --> 00:30:38,960
He was buried and he raised again and he has purchased our adoption into the family of God.

472
00:30:40,860 --> 00:30:42,900
This is the message of Jesus.

473
00:30:42,900 --> 00:30:46,560
And I can't help but think about Philip sharing this message with the eunuch.

474
00:30:46,560 --> 00:30:52,540
But not only does he share the message of Jesus, the spiritual realities of the message of Jesus,

475
00:30:52,540 --> 00:30:56,060
that all of these things are true, that Jesus has died in our place for our sins,

476
00:30:56,060 --> 00:31:01,680
and that he has purchased for us the opportunity to be part of the family of God.

477
00:31:01,740 --> 00:31:06,620
Not only does he share that, but he also shares the practical message of Jesus.

478
00:31:06,620 --> 00:31:12,880
See, on the same scroll, in Isaiah 55, verses 1 and 2,

479
00:31:12,880 --> 00:31:16,780
I can help but think Philip sharing this passage with the eunuch.

480
00:31:16,780 --> 00:31:17,140
It says,

481
00:31:17,140 --> 00:31:18,620
Is any of you thirsty?

482
00:31:18,620 --> 00:31:20,800
Come and drink.

483
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:25,740
Even if you have no money, come and take your choice of wine or milk.

484
00:31:25,740 --> 00:31:27,140
It's all free.

485
00:31:27,140 --> 00:31:31,100
Why spend your money on food that does not give you strength?

486
00:31:31,240 --> 00:31:33,800
Why pay for food that does you no good?

487
00:31:33,800 --> 00:31:37,160
Listen to me, and you will eat what is good.

488
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:39,180
You will enjoy the finest.

489
00:31:39,180 --> 00:31:44,500
The message of Jesus isn't just a spiritual reality for your eternity.

490
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:47,040
It's a practical reality for your today.

491
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:52,620
How many of us are tired and worn down from investing in temporary things?

492
00:31:52,620 --> 00:31:59,180
How many of us have been trying to chase success and fulfillment in temporary things?

493
00:31:59,780 --> 00:32:08,240
And successes at work in creating the perfect image of the perfect family that we can put on social media so everybody else can be jealous of the life that we lead.

494
00:32:08,240 --> 00:32:12,320
We know how exhausting it is to invest in temporary things.

495
00:32:12,320 --> 00:32:16,140
And the message of Jesus isn't just spiritual realities for eternity.

496
00:32:16,140 --> 00:32:18,100
It's practical realities for today.

497
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:21,960
Come today and drink from the well that is Jesus.

498
00:32:21,960 --> 00:32:24,280
He offers us abundant life.

499
00:32:24,280 --> 00:32:27,180
He offers us a well that will never run dry.

500
00:32:27,180 --> 00:32:38,280
Even when we find ourselves in those valleys in life, we can cling to the fact that Jesus brings us an abundant life.

501
00:32:39,700 --> 00:32:44,320
And then the next chapter over, the same scroll, Isaiah 56.

502
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:46,160
How practical is this for the eunuch?

503
00:32:46,160 --> 00:32:48,900
Isaiah 56, verses 3 through 5.

504
00:32:48,900 --> 00:32:49,300
It says,

505
00:32:50,160 --> 00:33:05,140
I will bless those eunuchs who keep my Sabbath days holy and who choose to do what pleases me and commit their lives to me.

506
00:33:05,140 --> 00:33:14,800
I will give them within the walls of my house a memorial and a name far greater than sons and daughters could give.

507
00:33:17,500 --> 00:33:23,000
See, the eunuch's riding home from spending some time worshiping in Jerusalem.

508
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,280
He's a seeker of God.

509
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:27,180
He wants a relationship with God.

510
00:33:27,180 --> 00:33:29,880
He wants to worship Yahweh, the one true God.

511
00:33:29,880 --> 00:33:33,680
But what happened to him in Jerusalem was he was forced to stay outside of the temple.

512
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:40,260
See, eunuchs weren't allowed to go into the temple because they had made decisions or decisions had been made for them that the Jews said,

513
00:33:40,260 --> 00:33:41,580
You're not welcome here.

514
00:33:41,580 --> 00:33:45,860
You can come kind of close to God, but you're not welcome inside his house.

515
00:33:46,060 --> 00:33:47,700
You're not welcome inside his doors.

516
00:33:47,700 --> 00:33:54,660
Because they had created a religion that was about decisions and choices rather than faith and trust in God.

517
00:33:54,660 --> 00:33:59,380
And Isaiah, 700 years before this man would go to Jerusalem to worship God, says,

518
00:33:59,380 --> 00:34:06,240
Listen, I know you've been told you're a dried up tree with no children and no future, but that's not what God says about you.

519
00:34:08,920 --> 00:34:16,680
The world might look at you and say, you've gone too far, you've messed up too much, you've made too many bad decisions, or too many people have treated you poorly.

520
00:34:16,680 --> 00:34:18,260
You're not worth the love of God.

521
00:34:18,260 --> 00:34:20,000
But that message is not true.

522
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:21,980
This is what God says about you.

523
00:34:22,880 --> 00:34:28,840
I will give you a memorial and a name that is far greater than any family lineage that you can leave.

524
00:34:28,840 --> 00:34:33,640
This is the message of gospel.

525
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:35,600
The gospel is true for us.

526
00:34:36,560 --> 00:34:43,880
That no matter where we've gone, no matter what decisions we've made, no matter what decisions other people have made for us or against us,

527
00:34:43,880 --> 00:34:47,080
no matter how much we might feel like damaged goods,

528
00:34:50,280 --> 00:34:53,940
God has given us an opportunity to be adopted into his family.

529
00:34:53,940 --> 00:35:03,940
And the legacy that we can leave by being a part of his spiritual family is far greater than any legacy we can leave by being successful at work

530
00:35:03,940 --> 00:35:08,220
or building a good life, a happy life with our family, or any of those things.

531
00:35:08,220 --> 00:35:13,300
We invest in the kingdom of God, and that kingdom produces eternal results.

532
00:35:14,360 --> 00:35:18,580
So if you're tired of trying to find success and satisfaction in this world,

533
00:35:18,580 --> 00:35:23,660
if you feel broken down because of decisions you've made or decisions other people have made for you,

534
00:35:23,660 --> 00:35:31,160
I believe God brought you here today to hear the same message that Philip shared with the eunuch 2,000 years ago.

535
00:35:31,160 --> 00:35:33,200
Jesus has bought for you an inheritance.

536
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:39,660
He has initiated an adoption into his family, and all you have to do is believe it.

537
00:35:39,660 --> 00:35:41,740
Accept it. Receive it.

538
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:50,300
So baptism starts with an obedient believer, and it's built on a clear understanding of the message of Jesus.

539
00:35:50,300 --> 00:35:55,360
And then it ends with an obedient believer.

540
00:35:55,360 --> 00:36:03,580
So Philip is in this chariot, hearing the message of Jesus clearly preached to him from Isaiah 53.

541
00:36:03,580 --> 00:36:06,040
And in verse 34, it says,

542
00:36:06,040 --> 00:36:09,320
The eunuch asked Philip, tell me what the prophet is telling him about.

543
00:36:10,020 --> 00:36:14,080
So beginning with this same Scripture, Philip told him the good news about Jesus.

544
00:36:14,080 --> 00:36:17,940
And as they rode along, they came to some water.

545
00:36:17,940 --> 00:36:21,900
And the eunuch said, look, there's some water.

546
00:36:21,900 --> 00:36:25,280
What is keeping me from being baptized?

547
00:36:25,280 --> 00:36:29,760
So he ordered the carriage to stop, and they went down into the water.

548
00:36:29,760 --> 00:36:32,640
And Philip baptized him.

549
00:36:35,640 --> 00:36:43,100
See, baptism starts with an obedient believer, a believer who's willing to step out and to do the thing that God's calling him to do.

550
00:36:43,100 --> 00:36:49,820
It's built on the message of Jesus, and it ends then with a new believer stepping out and saying,

551
00:36:49,820 --> 00:36:53,040
It's time for me to follow the way of Jesus.

552
00:36:53,040 --> 00:36:57,500
It's time for me to start this process of doing the thing that God has called me to do.

553
00:36:57,600 --> 00:36:59,940
Baptism ends with an obedient believer.

554
00:36:59,940 --> 00:37:03,640
Baptism is the culmination of the believer's obedience.

555
00:37:03,640 --> 00:37:10,040
See, in Jewish times, baptism was something that they were very familiar with.

556
00:37:10,040 --> 00:37:13,280
It was kind of just like a Facebook status, really.

557
00:37:13,280 --> 00:37:17,000
Like, they would get baptized whenever they decided they were going to fast,

558
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:18,780
or whenever they decided that they were going to go on.

559
00:37:18,980 --> 00:37:22,100
Like, it was just a way of telling people, I'm doing this thing,

560
00:37:22,100 --> 00:37:25,940
and so they're setting up this monument to tell people, this is what I'm doing.

561
00:37:25,940 --> 00:37:29,580
And so I think the eunuch was familiar with this process.

562
00:37:29,580 --> 00:37:32,100
He said, I want to follow this man in Jesus.

563
00:37:32,100 --> 00:37:35,160
I want to believe that he's the Messiah, the one who God said.

564
00:37:35,160 --> 00:37:40,960
I want to set up this monument to tell everyone around that I am going to follow Jesus.

565
00:37:42,760 --> 00:37:47,760
So what we say at Canvas a lot is that baptism is a picture of past salvation.

566
00:37:47,760 --> 00:37:54,040
We model the gospel as we are buried underwater and raised to new life.

567
00:37:54,040 --> 00:37:55,380
It's a picture of our salvation.

568
00:37:55,380 --> 00:37:57,360
It's a step of present obedience.

569
00:37:57,360 --> 00:37:58,140
We're doing the thing.

570
00:37:58,140 --> 00:38:01,960
We're going in front of people and making this public declaration,

571
00:38:01,960 --> 00:38:04,100
and it's a monument for future doubt.

572
00:38:04,100 --> 00:38:08,240
Like, we're telling everybody right here, right now, I'm doing this thing.

573
00:38:08,240 --> 00:38:10,200
I'm going to follow Jesus.

574
00:38:10,200 --> 00:38:15,780
And when the world beats us down, when we find ourselves in the valley struggling to follow Jesus

575
00:38:15,780 --> 00:38:18,400
and thinking, man, is this whole thing even real?

576
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:23,740
We can look back to the moment where we came in front of people and declared publicly for the first time,

577
00:38:23,740 --> 00:38:29,780
I am going to follow Jesus no matter how hard it might be, no matter how difficult it might be.

578
00:38:29,780 --> 00:38:31,300
I'm going to follow Jesus.

579
00:38:31,300 --> 00:38:37,200
This baptism is a culmination of a believer's obedience.

580
00:38:37,200 --> 00:38:39,380
I was thinking about how to illustrate this.

581
00:38:40,200 --> 00:38:44,800
And a couple weeks ago, Marissa and I, the kids are going to come in to wash some dots, which is awesome.

582
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:50,500
A couple weeks ago, Marissa and I planted a couple of herbs.

583
00:38:50,500 --> 00:38:56,160
And I'm not an agriculturalist by any stretch of the imagination.

584
00:38:56,160 --> 00:39:02,140
I don't know that I've ever successfully planted any plant and kept it alive.

585
00:39:02,580 --> 00:39:03,780
But we did this thing, right?

586
00:39:03,780 --> 00:39:10,440
We went to the greenhouse, and we got seeds, and we got soil, and we got buckets, and we got water.

587
00:39:10,440 --> 00:39:13,740
And we planted the seed.

588
00:39:13,740 --> 00:39:20,900
And throughout the last couple of weeks, we've adjusted where the bucket is so that it gets enough sunlight.

589
00:39:20,900 --> 00:39:22,980
And we've put water in the dirt.

590
00:39:22,980 --> 00:39:24,200
And we're doing these things.

591
00:39:24,200 --> 00:39:25,540
We're planting these seeds.

592
00:39:25,720 --> 00:39:35,560
We're making these steps in order for one day, in order so that one day we can see that stalk, that green plant, poke up out of the ground.

593
00:39:38,220 --> 00:39:41,600
I think that's such a good picture of what we see in baptism.

594
00:39:41,600 --> 00:40:00,220
See, this decision for somebody to go public with their faith, it's built on hundreds of believers' little decisions to step out of their comfort zone and to plant gospel seeds, to water gospel seeds, to encourage people to follow Jesus.

595
00:40:02,780 --> 00:40:07,040
And this baptism that we're going to celebrate, it's just the first fruit.

596
00:40:07,040 --> 00:40:12,940
It's just the green stalk poking out of the ground for the first time because following Jesus doesn't end with baptism.

597
00:40:12,940 --> 00:40:14,400
This is just the beginning.

598
00:40:14,400 --> 00:40:28,820
They're taking their first steps, and they're going to need us as a family of God to come around them and to disciple them and to encourage them to grow and to keep watering them and to keep putting them into sunlight and to keep putting opportunities for them to grow.

599
00:40:30,560 --> 00:40:39,820
So when we celebrate baptism, think about how many moments of obedience led to this point.

600
00:40:39,820 --> 00:40:44,720
Like, we're going to have a dad baptize his kids here in a minute.

601
00:40:44,720 --> 00:40:59,720
And all I can think about is how many times he was probably tired on his way home from church and didn't want to ask the kids about what they learned in their lesson, but maybe decided to push through that and have those conversations.

602
00:40:59,720 --> 00:41:07,800
I can't help but think about the hundreds of decisions that mom and dad made to have gospel conversations with their kids.

603
00:41:07,800 --> 00:41:16,920
I can't help but think of the hundreds of hours that Michelle put in to crafting the lessons for the kids so that they could hear about the message of Jesus and having conversations.

604
00:41:17,060 --> 00:41:24,140
I can't help but think about the small group leaders who take time out of their day to volunteer, to have conversation with the kids.

605
00:41:24,140 --> 00:41:28,740
Like, baptism is the culmination of a believer's obedience.

606
00:41:28,740 --> 00:41:36,260
Hundreds of little decisions and conversations happen behind the scenes to get us to this point, and this is just the beginning.

607
00:41:39,260 --> 00:41:49,380
So as we think about this fresh perspective on baptism, if you're an insider, if you've been around church for a long time, you've seen a lot of these.

608
00:41:50,380 --> 00:41:58,420
I hope this story from Philip and the eunuch reminds you that you could be a part of many more baptisms in the future.

609
00:41:58,420 --> 00:42:04,340
If you'd be willing to step out of your comfort zone, to be obedient, to do the thing that God's calling you to do.

610
00:42:04,540 --> 00:42:15,560
The greatest commission that Jesus gave us as believers is to go into all the world, preaching the good news of Jesus and baptizing people in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.

611
00:42:15,560 --> 00:42:17,640
You could be down here.

612
00:42:17,640 --> 00:42:20,720
Every week, if you want to, we'll fill up the tank every week.

613
00:42:20,720 --> 00:42:28,600
If you step out, if we step out and decide to be obedient, I can't imagine how many of these moments, like God set up for Philip,

614
00:42:28,600 --> 00:42:31,720
he's going to set up for us to have spiritual conversations with people in our lives.

615
00:42:33,220 --> 00:42:37,540
Now, if you're an outsider and you kind of think this whole thing is a little bit weird,

616
00:42:37,540 --> 00:42:47,940
I hope that you see that God is a very creative God, that he gave us pictures like communion and baptism,

617
00:42:47,940 --> 00:42:58,040
not just as a traditional ritual, but literally as a monument for us to remember what we're doing, what we're saying,

618
00:42:58,800 --> 00:43:04,420
that we are saying, that we're choosing to follow Jesus with our whole lives.

619
00:43:04,420 --> 00:43:09,020
And maybe you're here and you've been thinking about following Jesus for a long time,

620
00:43:09,020 --> 00:43:12,560
or you think that you are, but you've never gone public with it.

621
00:43:12,560 --> 00:43:16,880
You've never told people that you are today and moving forward going to follow Jesus.

622
00:43:18,160 --> 00:43:21,460
There's a table right here with towels, shorts, and shirts.

623
00:43:21,460 --> 00:43:23,560
If you want to get baptized, let's do it.

624
00:43:23,560 --> 00:43:25,440
There's nothing holding you back.

625
00:43:25,440 --> 00:43:26,640
I love what the eunuch says.

626
00:43:26,640 --> 00:43:28,740
He says, look, there's some water.

627
00:43:28,740 --> 00:43:30,520
What's keeping me from getting baptized?

628
00:43:30,520 --> 00:43:32,920
Same is true for you today.

629
00:43:32,920 --> 00:43:34,840
Look, there's some water.

630
00:43:34,840 --> 00:43:37,460
What's keeping you from getting baptized?

631
00:43:37,460 --> 00:43:41,300
In a few minutes, we're going to celebrate a couple of baptisms.

632
00:43:41,840 --> 00:43:44,340
And I love what the passage says.

633
00:43:44,340 --> 00:43:49,760
Philip disappears, and the eunuch goes on his way rejoicing.

634
00:43:49,760 --> 00:43:52,560
Baptism is a time for us to celebrate.

635
00:43:52,560 --> 00:43:54,860
It's a time for us to rejoice with those who are rejoicing.

636
00:43:54,860 --> 00:43:56,980
Let's get loud in this place when we baptize.

637
00:43:56,980 --> 00:44:03,060
Let's celebrate what God is doing in the lives of those around us.

638
00:44:04,500 --> 00:44:06,940
Baptism is the culmination of the believer's obedience.

639
00:44:06,940 --> 00:44:12,860
And nothing pleases the Father's heart more than an obedient believer.

640
00:44:12,860 --> 00:44:13,660
Let me pray.

641
00:44:13,660 --> 00:44:20,380
Father, we thank you so much for this opportunity to celebrate baptisms this morning.

642
00:44:20,380 --> 00:44:32,220
We pray that you would just honor the many obedient steps that were behind each of these baptisms,

643
00:44:32,620 --> 00:44:35,820
and that you would help us to realize that it's just the start of the process.

644
00:44:35,820 --> 00:44:41,620
That we continue to be obedient to disciples, to encourage, to point towards Jesus.

645
00:44:41,620 --> 00:44:46,020
God, thank you for the family of God.

646
00:44:46,020 --> 00:44:50,460
I pray that you would just honor the rest of our time this morning.

647
00:44:50,460 --> 00:44:52,500
In Jesus' name I pray.

648
00:44:52,500 --> 00:44:53,380
Amen.
